WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
What review costs, and how to budget it.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:07.064
Human review is the most expensive layer, so estimate it like any other cost.

3
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:13.680
The inputs are traces per batch, minutes per trace, batches per month, and the reviewer’s loaded hourly cost.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:26.120
A domain expert reading a RAG trace (question, answer, five chunks) and writing a one-line critique might take two to four minutes; an agent transcript with a dozen tool calls can take ten.

5
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.093
Measure your own number in the first session with your review tool, because it varies widely.

6
00:00:31.373 --> 00:00:40.717
A worked example with stated assumptions: 120 traces every two weeks, 3 minutes each, is 6 hours a batch, or about 12 hours a month.

7
00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:49.595
At a loaded cost of $100 an hour, that is $1,200 a month, likely more than the model spend on many internal tools.

8
00:00:49.875 --> 00:01:00.349
The comparison that matters is not review versus zero; it is review versus shipping undetected failures, and versus the judge-calibration labels you would need anyway.

9
00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:03.723
Three levers reduce the cost without losing the signal.

10
00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:08.099
Better tooling: one-screen context and hotkeys cut minutes per trace.

11
00:01:08.379 --> 00:01:17.552
Better sampling: once your judge is calibrated, let it screen everything and send humans the disagreements, the low-confidence cases, and the random slice.

12
00:01:17.832 --> 00:01:22.141
Saturation: stop a round when new traces stop teaching you anything new.

13
00:01:22.421 --> 00:01:27.925
What you should not cut is the random slice, because it is the only unbiased estimate you have.

14
00:01:28.205 --> 00:01:35.096
In a real team, put the review cadence on the calendar, name the owner, track hours, and report what each round found.

15
00:01:35.376 --> 00:01:42.309
A review program that produces a list of new failure modes and new test cases every two weeks justifies itself.
